All require retraining or weight modification; our method is a plug-in predictor on \emph{unmodified} SwiGLU models.

\paragraph{Weight Pruning.}
Wanda~\cite{sun2024wanda} prunes weights by magnitude$\times$activation norm without retraining; SparseGPT~\cite{frantar2023sparsegpt} solves layer-wise reconstruction for one-shot unstructured weight pruning.
Both produce static weight masks, while our method generates dynamic per-token activation masks that adapt to each input.

\paragraph{Conditional Computation and Routing.}
Switch Transformers~\cite{fedus2022switch} route tokens to sparse expert subsets via learned gating, achieving computational sparsity through architecture design.
Our work achieves analogous per-token computation reduction on dense pretrained models without architectural modification or expert retraining.

\paragraph{Knowledge Distillation for Compression.}
Sequence-level distillation~\cite{kim2016sequencekd} and DistilBERT~\cite{sanh2019distilbert} train compact student models to match teacher output distributions.
We apply the same distributional matching principle (KL divergence) not to a smaller model but to a sparsity predictor that determines which neurons to retain in the original model.

\paragraph{Relaxation Methods.}
Our Gumbel-Sigmoid mask sampling builds on the Concrete distribution~\cite{maddison2017concrete} and $L_0$ regularization~\cite{louizos2018l0}, which enable gradient-based optimization of discrete masks via continuous relaxation.
We adapt this technique from weight pruning to per-token activation prediction with a coupled (KL) rather than decomposed objective.

